//! lotus/stereo_soft_center_clip.rye — ALES142: the soft center clipper carried into stereo, ONE SHARED CONTINUOUS DEAD ZONE, the phase-holding yet ratio-breaking member of the stereo dead-zone corner. ALES88 named
//! `soft_center_clip` — silence the quiet middle AND shift every survivor toward zero by the threshold, y = if |x| ≤ t then 0 else x − sign(x)·t, so the survivor rises CONTINUOUSLY from zero (|x| = t+1 → ±1, not
//! ±(t+1)): the continuous twin of ALES141's hard center clip, which passed the loud UNCHANGED and left a JUMP at the boundary. It is the floor-side twin of the ceiling pair the DRIVE family carries — ALES132's hard
//! clip pins abruptly, ALES133's soft drive rounds the corner; ALES141's hard center clip jumps, this soft center clip is continuous. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose
//! defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length. This rung carries the soft center clip into stereo the plain way: run ALES88's proven mono `soft_center_clip` on each channel with the SAME threshold,
//! so one continuous dead zone lands on BOTH speakers together.
//!
//! The soft center clip is ODD (sign(−x)·t flips with the sign), so like ALES141 it HOLDS the inter-channel antisymmetry: an out-of-phase master (right the exact negation of left) comes back STILL out of phase. Yet
//! unlike the hard center clip it is a SHIFT, not a mask, and a shift is not scale-invariant, so the soft form BREAKS the inter-channel ratio — a 1:2 master does NOT stay 1:2, because subtracting the same t from both
//! channels' survivors pulls the quieter one proportionally harder (2x − t ≠ 2(x − t)). And it is NOT idempotent — each pass subtracts t again. This is the honest middle of the class: the hard center clip held both
//! ratio and antisymmetry and was idempotent; the soft center clip holds only the antisymmetry, stated plainly rather than dressed in a symmetry it lacks. What both members share, carried into stereo, is the SAME dead
//! zone and the soft/hard bond: on every survivor |soft| = |hard| − t, per channel — proven here against ALES141's stereo_center_clip.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: the soft center clip is EXACT for every i16 with NO rail edge and NO saturate — a positive survivor lands in [1, sample_max], a negative in [sample_min, −1], the dead zone at 0. The magnitude
//! is taken in i32 inside ALES88 so |sample_min| = 32768 is representable. It carries ONE parameter, the threshold magnitude, and names TWO faults. Refuse BadThreshold (a threshold outside [0, sample_max]) and BadRange
//! (a span outside the samples) BEFORE either channel is mutated — each fault depends only on the shared threshold or the shared length, so each check is made ONCE up front, and a refusal never soft-center-clips one
//! channel and leaves the other whole; otherwise soft_center_clip both channels over [start, start+count) with the same `thresh` via ALES88's `soft_center_clip`. SoftCenterClipError reused whole — the stereo lift adds
//! no fault.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO SOFT CENTER-CLIP LAW: each channel equals ALES88's mono `soft_center_clip` with the same threshold over the same span byte for byte — the quiet middle silenced, every survivor
//! shifted toward zero by t, the boundary continuous, no rail edge. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length (a soft center clip writes values only). THE
//! PER-CHANNEL ODD / SHIFT LAW: the soft center clip is odd, so a shared dead zone HOLDS the inter-channel antisymmetry (an out-of-phase master staying out of phase); yet it is a SHIFT, so it BREAKS the inter-channel
//! ratio (a 1:2 master not staying 1:2) and is NOT idempotent (twice ≠ once); an identical-channel master stays identical, thresh = 0 the identity, and the soft/hard bond holds per channel (|soft| = |hard| − t on every
//! survivor, against ALES141's stereo_center_clip). THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal (BadThreshold, BadRange) leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; thresh = 0 the identity on both,
//! count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES142.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-042641_fill-ales142-lotus-stereo-soft-center-clip.md
//! Stands on: lotus/soft_center_clip.rye (ALES88) — the mono soft center clipper run once per channel, y = if |x| ≤ t then 0 else x − sign(x)·t with no rail edge; lotus/stereo_center_clip.rye (ALES141) — the hard
//! sibling whose dead zone this shares and whose survivors this pulls toward zero by t (the soft/hard bond); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only through ALES88's own soft_center_clip, each output either
//! silence or a same-sign survivor pulled toward zero by exactly t, never a length; the shape is a static continuous dead-zone threshold on instantaneous magnitude, memoryless (no attack/release, no envelope, no
//! anti-aliasing). No saturate is owed — every output fits the i16 rail by construction. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_soft_center_clip.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const soft_center_clip = @import("soft_center_clip.rye");
const stereo_center_clip = @import("stereo_center_clip.rye");

pub const stereo_soft_center_clip_version = "20260815.042641";

/// Stereo soft-center-clip's faults are exactly ALES88's own `SoftCenterClipError` — `BadThreshold` (a threshold outside [0, sample_max]) and `BadRange` (a span outside the samples) — reused by name, since the stereo
/// lift adds no new fault: it only runs the same soft_center_clip on each channel after one shared validation.
pub const StereoSoftCenterClipError = soft_center_clip.SoftCenterClipError;

/// Soft-center-clip `[start, start+count)` in BOTH channels of `sc` with the same `thresh`, the SAME continuous dead zone on each, running ALES88's proven mono `soft_center_clip`. Each sample whose magnitude is at most
/// `thresh` becomes `0` (the quiet middle silenced), and every louder sample is shifted toward zero by `thresh` — y = if |x| ≤ thresh then 0 else x − sign(x)·thresh, the survivor rising continuously from zero. Exact
/// for every i16 with no rail edge, so no saturate is owed. `thresh = 0` is the identity. Validates both channels once against the shared threshold and length before either is mutated: refuses `BadThreshold` /
/// `BadRange`, both channels untouched. Values only — never the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_soft_center_clip(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32, thresh: i32) StereoSoftCenterClipError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the threshold and span are legal — refused by name before any write, so a refusal never soft-center-clips one channel and leaves the other whole. The threshold check depends only on the shared
    // magnitude bound; the span check on the shared length. These mirror ALES88's own guards exactly, so each mono soft_center_clip below is pre-validated to succeed.
    if (thresh < 0) return error.BadThreshold;
    if (thresh > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadThreshold;
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono soft_center_clip is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME continuous dead zone on each.
    try soft_center_clip.soft_center_clip(&sc.left, start, count, thresh);
    try soft_center_clip.soft_center_clip(&sc.right, start, count, thresh);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — a soft center clip writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SOFT CENTER-CLIP LAW — each channel equals ALES88's mono soft_center_clip with the same threshold over the same span byte for byte; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — left.len == right.len after,
//     both holding their starting length; THE PER-CHANNEL ODD / SHIFT LAW — an OUT-OF-PHASE master coming back STILL out of phase (odd holds the antisymmetry), a 1:2 master NOT staying 1:2 (the shift breaks the
//     ratio), the map NOT idempotent (twice ≠ once), an identical-channel master staying identical, thresh = 0 the identity, and the soft/hard bond |soft| = |hard| − t on every survivor against ALES141; THE ATOMICITY
//     / DEGENERATE LAW — each refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, thresh = 0 the identity, count = 0 the identity ----

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO SOFT CENTER-CLIP LAW — each channel equals ALES88's mono soft_center_clip with the same threshold, proven side by side over one span with different per-channel samples; at t = 1000 the quiet middle
    //    silences and every survivor is shifted toward zero by 1000.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 500, 1500, 3000, -1500, -3000 }, &[_]i16{ 100, -1200, 2500, -300, 5000 });
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&sc, 0, 5, 1000);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 500, 1500, 3000, -1500, -3000 });
        try soft_center_clip.soft_center_clip(&ml, 0, 5, 1000);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 100, -1200, 2500, -300, 5000 });
        try soft_center_clip.soft_center_clip(&mr, 0, 5, 1000);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // by hand: left 500→0, 1500→500, 3000→2000, −1500→−500, −3000→−2000; right 100→0, −1200→−200, 2500→1500, −300→0, 5000→4000.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 500, 2000, -500, -2000 })) return error.LeftHandWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, -200, 1500, 0, 4000 })) return error.RightHandWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a soft center clip writes values only. Proven at an even and an odd length.
    {
        var even = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&even, &[_]i16{ 100, -2000, 3000, -4000 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -200, 5000 });
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&even, 0, 4, 1000);
        if (even.left.len != even.right.len) return error.EvenUnbalanced;
        if (even.left.len != 4) return error.EvenLength;

        var odd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&odd, &[_]i16{ 100, -2000, 3000 }, &[_]i16{ 3000, -200, 100 });
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&odd, 0, 3, 500);
        if (odd.left.len != odd.right.len) return error.OddUnbalanced;
        if (odd.left.len != 3) return error.OddLength;
    }

    // 3a. THE PER-CHANNEL ODD LAW, the ANTISYMMETRY HOLDS — an out-of-phase master (right the exact negation of left) soft-center-clipped through the same threshold comes back STILL out of phase, because the soft
    //     dead-zone map is odd (sign(−x)·t flips with the sign), so soft_center_clip(−x) = −soft_center_clip(x) and right = −left after. The phase is held, like the hard center clip.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 900, -1200 }, &[_]i16{ -500, 3000, -900, 1200 }); // right = −left
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&sc, 0, 4, 1000);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, r[i]) != -@as(i32, l[i])) return error.AntisymmetryBroke; // right = −left held — the odd law in stereo
        }
        // by hand: |500|,|900| ≤ 1000 silence; 3000→2000, 1200→200. left {0,−2000,0,−200}, right {0,2000,0,200}.
        if (!eq(l, &[_]i16{ 0, -2000, 0, -200 })) return error.LeftPhaseWrong;
        if (!eq(r, &[_]i16{ 0, 2000, 0, 200 })) return error.RightPhaseWrong;
    }

    // 3b. THE SHIFT LAW, the RATIO BREAKS — a 1:2 master (right twice left) does NOT stay 1:2, because the soft center clip is a SHIFT and a shift is not scale-invariant: subtracting the same t from both survivors
    //     pulls the quieter channel proportionally harder (2x − t ≠ 2(x − t)). This is the honest edge the hard center clip did not have. Under a shift the ratio must break where both survive.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 2000, 3000 }, &[_]i16{ 4000, 6000 }); // right = 2·left, both survive t = 1000
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&sc, 0, 2, 1000);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        // by hand: left 2000→1000, 3000→2000; right 4000→3000, 6000→5000. 2·1000 = 2000 ≠ 3000 — the ratio broke.
        if (!eq(l, &[_]i16{ 1000, 2000 })) return error.RatioLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(r, &[_]i16{ 3000, 5000 })) return error.RatioRightWrong;
        if (@as(i32, r[0]) == 2 * @as(i32, l[0])) return error.RatioUnexpectedlyHeld; // the 1:2 does NOT hold — that is the point
    }

    // 3c. THE SHIFT LAW, NOT IDEMPOTENT — each pass subtracts t again (a shift, not a mask), so soft-center-clipping twice differs from once on both channels wherever a survivor survives the second pass. Proven
    //     honestly against ALES141's idempotence: this rung does NOT have it.
    {
        const li = [_]i16{ 5000, -6000, 4000 };
        const ri = [_]i16{ -7000, 8000, -3000 };
        var once = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&once, &li, &ri);
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&once, 0, 3, 1000);
        var twice = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&twice, &li, &ri);
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&twice, 0, 3, 1000);
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&twice, 0, 3, 1000);
        if (eq(once.left.samples(), twice.left.samples()) and eq(once.right.samples(), twice.right.samples())) return error.UnexpectedlyIdempotent;
        // by hand once: left 5000→4000, −6000→−5000, 4000→3000; twice: 4000→3000, −5000→−4000, 3000→2000.
        if (!eq(twice.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 3000, -4000, 2000 })) return error.TwiceLeftWrong;
    }

    // 3d. THE SOFT/HARD BOND — the soft center clip (this rung) and ALES141's hard center clip silence the SAME dead zone, and on every survivor the soft output is exactly t quieter in magnitude: |soft| = |hard| − t,
    //     per channel per sample. The family read across the two dead-zone members in stereo.
    {
        const li = [_]i16{ 300, -300, 1200, -1200, 5000, -5000 };
        const ri = [_]i16{ -900, 900, -3000, 3000, 700, -700 };
        const t: i32 = 1000;
        var soft = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&soft, &li, &ri);
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&soft, 0, 6, t);
        var hard = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&hard, &li, &ri);
        try stereo_center_clip.stereo_center_clip(&hard, 0, 6, t);
        const sl = soft.left.samples();
        const sr = soft.right.samples();
        const hl = hard.left.samples();
        const hr = hard.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 6) : (i += 1) {
            // left channel
            if (hl[i] == 0) {
                if (sl[i] != 0) return error.LeftDeadZoneDiffers; // same samples silenced
            } else {
                const sm: i32 = if (sl[i] < 0) -@as(i32, sl[i]) else @as(i32, sl[i]);
                const hm: i32 = if (hl[i] < 0) -@as(i32, hl[i]) else @as(i32, hl[i]);
                if (sm != hm - t) return error.LeftBondWrong; // |soft| = |hard| − t
            }
            // right channel
            if (hr[i] == 0) {
                if (sr[i] != 0) return error.RightDeadZoneDiffers;
            } else {
                const sm: i32 = if (sr[i] < 0) -@as(i32, sr[i]) else @as(i32, sr[i]);
                const hm: i32 = if (hr[i] < 0) -@as(i32, hr[i]) else @as(i32, hr[i]);
                if (sm != hm - t) return error.RightBondWrong;
            }
        }
    }

    // 3e. an identical-channel master stays identical through the soft center clip — a shared dead zone and shift on equal inputs gives equal outputs (a mono-in-stereo signal never splits).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 }, &[_]i16{ 500, -3000, 500, -3000 });
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&sc, 0, 4, 1000);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 3f. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE — soft-center-clipping a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were on both channels (the in-place discipline the whole suite keeps).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 500, 5000, 300, 400 }, &[_]i16{ -400, -300, 500, -5000, 300, 400 });
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&sc, 2, 2, 1000); // indices 2,3 only, both channels: 500→0, 5000→4000 / 500→0, −5000→−4000
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 0, 4000, 300, 400 })) return error.LeftSpanLeaked;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -400, -300, 0, -4000, 300, 400 })) return error.RightSpanLeaked;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad threshold — a negative threshold and an over-rail threshold each refuse BadThreshold, both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, -2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ -3, 2, -1 });
        if (stereo_soft_center_clip(&sc, 0, 3, -1)) |_| return error.NegThreshSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongNegThresh;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, -2, 3 })) return error.NegThreshLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -3, 2, -1 })) return error.NegThreshRightMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.NegThreshUnbalanced;

        var over = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&over, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 3, 2, 1 });
        if (stereo_soft_center_clip(&over, 0, 3, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1)) |_| return error.OverThreshSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongOverThresh;
        if (!eq(over.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.OverThreshLeftMutated;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad span — a span past the samples refuses BadRange, both channels untouched.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 });
        if (stereo_soft_center_clip(&sc, 1, 3, 1000)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 })) return error.BadRangeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 })) return error.BadRangeRightMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE DEGENERATE LAW — thresh = 0 is the identity on both channels (nothing silenced, nothing subtracted), and count = 0 is the identity on both.
    {
        var zero = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zero, &[_]i16{ 401, -299, 703 }, &[_]i16{ -701, 201, -401 });
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&zero, 0, 3, 0); // identity — no dead zone, no shift
        if (!eq(zero.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 401, -299, 703 })) return error.ZeroThreshLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(zero.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -701, 201, -401 })) return error.ZeroThreshRightChanged;

        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 });
        try stereo_soft_center_clip(&noop, 1, 0, 1000); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-soft-center-clip: the SOFT CENTER CLIPPER CARRIED INTO STEREO, ONE SHARED CONTINUOUS DEAD ZONE, the phase-holding yet ratio-breaking member of the stereo dead-zone corner — soft-center-" ++
        "clip a span in both channels of a master with the same threshold, running ALES88's proven mono soft_center_clip on each (y = if |x| ≤ t then 0 else x − sign(x)·t, the quiet middle silenced and every survivor " ++
        "shifted toward zero by t, the survivor rising continuously from zero, no rail edge and no saturate), so one continuous dead zone lands on both speakers together. A Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose " ++
        "defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. It is the continuous twin of ALES141's hard center clip, which jumped at the boundary. The soft center clip is ODD (sign(−x)·t flips with the " ++
        "sign), so it HOLDS the inter-channel antisymmetry (an out-of-phase master staying out of phase), like the hard center clip; yet unlike it the soft form is a SHIFT, not a mask, so it BREAKS the inter-channel " ++
        "ratio (a 1:2 master not staying 1:2, since 2x − t ≠ 2(x − t)) and is NOT idempotent (each pass subtracts t again) — the honest middle of the class, stated plainly rather than dressed in a symmetry it lacks. " ++
        "What both dead-zone members share is the SAME dead zone and the soft/hard bond: on every survivor |soft| = |hard| − t, per channel, proven here against ALES141's stereo_center_clip. An identical-channel " ++
        "master stays identical and thresh = 0 is the identity. It validates both channels once against the shared threshold and length before either is mutated (BadThreshold outside [0, sample_max], BadRange on a " ++
        "span past the samples), so a refusal never soft-center-clips one channel and leaves the other whole; SoftCenterClipError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO SOFT CENTER-CLIP LAW: each channel " ++
        "equals ALES88's mono soft_center_clip with the same threshold byte for byte. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after and both hold their starting length. THE PER-CHANNEL ODD / SHIFT LAW: the " ++
        "antisymmetry held (an out-of-phase master staying out of phase), the ratio broken (a 1:2 master not staying 1:2), the map not idempotent, an identical master staying identical, thresh = 0 the identity, the " ++
        "soft/hard bond |soft| = |hard| − t per survivor. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, thresh = 0 the identity, count = 0 the identity. Purely local — two " ++
        "bounded i16 Clips soft-center-clipped through the proven mono soft_center_clip, a static continuous dead-zone threshold on instantaneous magnitude, memoryless; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no " ++
        "funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
